# Pull requests

When opening PRs for this repository (including via `gh pr create`):

## Titles

- Use **conventional commits** style: `type(scope): short description` (same spirit as merge commits).
- **Subject must not start with uppercase** — CI `semantic-pull-request` enforces
  `subjectPattern: ^(?![A-Z]).+$` (see `.github/workflows/conventional-commits.yml`).
  The part after `: ` starts lowercase even when the words include proper nouns later
  (for example `Guided`, `Ansible`, `Terraform`).
  - Good: `feat(portal): add Guided/Advanced forms for Terraform and Ansible`
  - Bad: `feat(portal): Guided/Advanced forms for Terraform and Ansible`
- Prefer matching the primary commit subject when opening the PR (`gh pr create --title`
  is linted separately from commitlint — a valid commit does not fix a bad PR title).
- Do **not** put **roadmap theme labels** or **unreleased product milestone numbers** in the title
  (for example avoid `v1.19`, `v1.18 portal`, `v1.25` as the feature name).
  Roadmap versions describe planning themes; GitHub **engine tags** (for example `v1.25.0`) come from
  semantic-release and may not match roadmap numbering.
- OK in titles: semver when it is the **actual artifact** being changed (for example
  `chore(release): 1.26.0` on Release automation PRs).

## Bodies

- Summarize user-visible behavior and link operator/portal/docs impacts when relevant.
- Test plan checklist; note if operator JSON contracts (`plan-upgrade`, `apply-upgrade`) are unchanged.
- Blueprint or catalog emit changes: confirm `make blueprint-conformance-check` passed
  (or list updated `conformance.manifest*.json` files).

## Roadmap header

- Do **not** edit `docs/roadmap.md` **Current release** — Release/`sync_doc_versions.py` owns it.
- Keep a blank line after **Current release** so feature edits to **In progress** / **Shipped**
  do not conflict with release bumps.
- Feature PRs may update **In progress**, **Shipped on `main`**, and section `**Status:**` only.

## Roadmap housecleaning (same PR as shipped work)

When a PR lands feature work, include doc housekeeping — **not** `make chart-validate`
unless the diff also touches `deploy/k8s/chart/**`.

Checklist:

1. **Header** — **In progress** / **Shipped on `main`** reflect what merged.
2. **Theme table + path tree** — open → shipped/partial where applicable.
3. **Section status** — every touched theme has an accurate `**Status:**` line.
4. **Stale text** — remove wrong branch names, `Not started`, superseded “blocked behind” notes.
5. **Cross-links** — update `docs/durability.md`, `docs/verify.md`, operations runbooks,
   and k8s READMEs when operators need the new paths.

Docs-only housecleaning commits: skip the Python quality loop; see
`.cursor/rules/pre-commit.mdc`.

## Merging to `main`

After required checks pass, use the normal **Merge pull request** control on GitHub.
Release automation (`chore/release/*`) still admin-merges outside branch rules.
See [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md) and [`.github/rulesets/README.md`](../.github/rulesets/README.md).

## PR URLs

Use the **GitHub PR URL** from `gh pr view --json url` (for example
`https://github.com/opsdevcode/repave/pull/445`). Do **not** substitute Cursor
autopilot or cloud-agent review links (`[Review](bc-id#changes)`, `[Try Live]`, etc.)
unless the user explicitly asks for autopilot.

## After opening a PR

When `gh pr create` succeeds (or the user asked to open a PR and one already exists),
**always end the turn with merge status and a detailed “What’s next” section** — do not
stop at the PR URL alone.

### 1. Verify merge readiness

Run for the PR just opened (and note conflicts on related open PRs):

```bash
gh pr view <number> --json number,title,url,mergeable,mergeStateStatus,state
gh pr list --state open --json number,title,headRefName,mergeable,mergeStateStatus
```

Report explicitly:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `mergeable: MERGEABLE` | No merge conflicts with base |
| `mergeable: CONFLICTING` | **Conflicts** — rebase on `origin/main` before merge |
| `mergeable: UNKNOWN` | GitHub still computing — say so |
| `mergeStateStatus: BLOCKED` | Normal when required checks are pending or failing |
| `mergeStateStatus: DIRTY` | Conflicts or git error state |

If **CONFLICTING** / **DIRTY**, say so first and offer to rebase/fix — do not describe
the PR as merge-ready.

Optionally summarize CI: `gh pr checks <number>` or `statusCheckRollup` from
`gh pr view --json statusCheckRollup`.

### 2. Gather context

- Read **`docs/roadmap.md`** — top **In progress** / **Shipped** lines plus the section
  that matches the work (service decomposition, durability, operator, portal, etc.).
- Read **`docs/adr/`** when the next item references an ADR.
- List **other open PRs** (`gh pr list --state open`) that share the theme or should
  merge before/after the new PR.

### 3. Display “What’s next” (detailed)

Use this structure (adjust length to the task; skip empty subsections):

**Merge status** — one line per open PR you touched: URL, mergeable/conflicts, CI summary.

**Recommended order** — numbered list of what to do before starting new work (for example
wait for CI, merge #321, merge #322, pull `main`).

**Next implementation** (primary follow-up task):

- **Goal** — one sentence from the roadmap problem/approach
- **Scope** — key paths, packages, or workflows to touch
- **Done when** — acceptance criteria from roadmap or ADR
- **Depends on** — merged PRs, infra, or prior phases
- **Start with** — suggested branch name and first command or test to run

**Later / optional** — deferred roadmap items (Phase 4, sessions store, etc.) only when
relevant; keep short.

Name concrete next tasks the user can request (for example “decomposed chart CI smoke”),
not “let me know if you want more.”
